Same as the DBCP one: `serverAddress` can be a literal IPv6 address, and appending `:port` straight onto it gives `::1:5432`, which reads as a bare address rather than address + port. Worth bracketing here, same as `UrlParsingUtils.buildShortUrl` does when it builds `db.connection_string`.

```suggestion
    StringBuilder poolName = new StringBuilder();
    if (serverAddress != null) {
      if (serverAddress.indexOf(':') >= 0) {
        poolName.append('[').append(serverAddress).append(']');
      } else {
        poolName.append(serverAddress);
      }
      if (serverPort != null) {
        poolName.append(':').append(serverPort);
      }
    }
```

This depends on #19366 landing first, which normalizes `getServerAddress()` to always be unbracketed. Today it's already bracketed for some drivers, so the plain colon check would double-bracket those.

Could you also add a name-derivation test with an IPv6 URL, e.g. `jdbc:postgresql://[2001:db8::1]:5432/orders` -> `[2001:db8::1]:5432/orders`?

